#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188204 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc062b6-306b-5edc-81db-1a3051cf3f51" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "4b196d96-a95f-53d6-a0c5-7233de14a401" :question/math-academy-id "e-5445" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3 \\to \\mathbb{R}^3.$ If $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=1,$ then what is $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n,$ then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) + \\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given transformation, we have $n=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(T)+1 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(T) & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188207 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "0eddf8b0-bdd9-55db-9388-d39bd91c24c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7251" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^4 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^4$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^4.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$, then $\\textrm{__________}.$ *\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\textrm{rank}(T)=3$\n\nII. $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=3$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^4$ into $\\mathbb{R}^4,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) ![](images/5d/5d330995eaf0f71f9006f897a75e9e892caecb94eb8c8b48d7ba7f116633b6e3.svg) \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) + \\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = 4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ and the set $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\textrm{rank}(T)=\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =4-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq 4$ (or since $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq 0$), the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188216 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a9a16cc-d807-5015-ba40-b4a15fe3a0db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Given a Description of the Image or Kernel"}] :question/id #uuid "91885df0-9f1d-5c18-acf1-70259cf7f4d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9987" :question/problem "Find $\\text{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})$ given that $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3\\to\\mathbb{R}^3$ and $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\{ [ x, y, z ]^T \\, : \\, x=y=z\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Any vector from $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ x \\\\ x\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}.$ Therefore, $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=1.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ec4bc02-0df9-5348-9ee4-473b64cacd02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472718} {:answer/id #uuid "0b06b982-fb9d-5cf4-b752-4ccd290073ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b06b982-fb9d-5cf4-b752-4ccd290073ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472719} :answer-field/id #uuid "edc239d3-5a4c-5fa5-98b1-768823c16b23" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473754} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72dd48fb-940a-538d-8ccc-7a6e5aa609a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472720} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd1213a-a7a4-5890-88d8-8de888db8343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72dd48fb-940a-538d-8ccc-7a6e5aa609a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472720} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ff1d1ba-36f4-5b99-8a47-88e5119e65f7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473755} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05116011-77ca-56fd-9a97-eba1d5a68cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472722} {:answer/id #uuid "01638de6-6ede-5e18-812c-b75ad72b29e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05116011-77ca-56fd-9a97-eba1d5a68cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472722} :answer-field/id #uuid "dec578f4-3243-5145-b140-1c5ad943604d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b72abcb6-85da-5c4d-a8eb-72ce20ae6b72" :question/math-academy-id "q-140100" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in ${R}^{5}.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$, then $\\text{__________}.$ * \n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(T)=4$\n- **II.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=4$\n- **III.** $T$ is a bijection\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=5.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =5-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =5-4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc062b6-306b-5edc-81db-1a3051cf3f51" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5364440e-35ab-5352-9bd1-0884f1fa53aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365075} {:answer/id #uuid "8d85edbd-a7fc-55ed-a81f-ec06151240f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365076} {:answer/id #uuid "6f432cea-8a38-5640-97df-08aac8c68621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365077} {:answer/id #uuid "5f813b7b-ad83-5cfd-8d75-d5120f30bfad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365078} {:answer/id #uuid "3911bb66-4769-5470-b6cf-ec8614901d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5364440e-35ab-5352-9bd1-0884f1fa53aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365075} :answer-field/id #uuid "76b710e0-0e91-5d91-9b48-e0878a4a3818" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb42efe7-06d5-5703-9693-8ecd2e6793ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-141426" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}.$ If $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given transformation, we have $n=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(T)+2 & =5 \\\\ \\text{rank}(T) & =5-2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365080 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc062b6-306b-5edc-81db-1a3051cf3f51" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31a58cc7-d8df-569e-9b11-77aa6503eca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365082} {:answer/id #uuid "c98745e4-a910-53c6-953f-ad94a707f469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365083} {:answer/id #uuid "effc1519-26ea-5317-b541-492633bbee19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365084} {:answer/id #uuid "78bf044e-f33e-5626-a12a-efc1c0b7e171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365085} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2fba1e-99bf-56f5-96bc-1c30b786a760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78bf044e-f33e-5626-a12a-efc1c0b7e171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365085} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fdbbe34-84b9-57ee-be72-e01c0e39083a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93ca63e2-f868-5041-861e-481f25303fb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-141429" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T:{R}^{10}\\to {R}^{10}.$ If $\\text{rank}(T)=6$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given transformation, we have $n=10.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T) & =n \\\\ 6+\\text{nullity}(T) & =10 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(T) & =10-6 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc062b6-306b-5edc-81db-1a3051cf3f51" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f43a18-d791-5a02-801f-df3387b654f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492228} {:answer/id #uuid "6a246604-aaa7-55b5-bab5-a4f06f9cea5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492428} {:answer/id #uuid "b3e0c222-dde9-50f4-9203-5e6c0c0f12c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492576} {:answer/id #uuid "d2566bb9-7c8f-5410-8d5b-d5a4770b8c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492632} {:answer/id #uuid "f9bcdef5-3329-5d37-abe0-e83d65624660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2566bb9-7c8f-5410-8d5b-d5a4770b8c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492632} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7254d97-c107-5cd3-ac3d-c4b19a9b8902" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f1a69d2-da4d-539f-85b3-5e00cfab3ac7" :question/math-academy-id "q-141430" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}.$ If $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given transformation, we have $n=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T) & =n \\\\ 1+\\text{nullity}(T) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(T) & =5-1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a9a16cc-d807-5015-ba40-b4a15fe3a0db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Given a Description of the Image or Kernel"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ecb0cc3-afd8-5a46-bd9d-b371a0254225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492278} {:answer/id #uuid "39053542-c293-5a9e-ac44-450935d27710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492338} {:answer/id #uuid "9f354c6d-accd-573b-b23d-3f21af38a5ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492530} {:answer/id #uuid "a71d7da3-cf8a-575a-8186-3190e30287fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492554} {:answer/id #uuid "ee0ce244-827d-597e-bf10-65d70b6ac24b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f354c6d-accd-573b-b23d-3f21af38a5ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492530} :answer-field/id #uuid "df557d07-88b6-5980-91f5-1e6a3fe0da71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c619e065-6d72-5648-a786-860dc455f368" :question/math-academy-id "q-141434" :question/problem "Find $\\text{nullity}(T)$ given that $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ and $\\text{Im}(T)=\\{[x,y,z,u,v]^{T}\\,:\\,x-2y=0,\\,3u+v=0\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Solving $x-2y=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=2y.$ Solving $3u+v=0$ for $v,$ we get $v=-3u.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2y \\\\ y \\\\ z \\\\ u \\\\ -3u\\end{aligned}]=y{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}+u{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=3.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =5-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =5-3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a9a16cc-d807-5015-ba40-b4a15fe3a0db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Given a Description of the Image or Kernel"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eb9bce1-0297-53c0-a167-65429a68ca75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492242} {:answer/id #uuid "18bfa1fb-ec39-5e06-aa2f-27a24093c541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492263} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4a310e-9fad-5e19-bae2-5bbe90f27a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492318} {:answer/id #uuid "77afe151-5f72-5b97-89d9-53d0a85f7dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492458} {:answer/id #uuid "d70a1b7e-3b63-5d89-abf8-bbeae48c9ac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77afe151-5f72-5b97-89d9-53d0a85f7dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492458} :answer-field/id #uuid "873870cb-4f04-54ab-9bd6-a01e9f25ce5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a90e4a0d-4779-5458-9918-a30c4fa7517b" :question/math-academy-id "q-141437" :question/problem "Find $\\text{rank}(T)$ given that $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ and $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{[x,y,z,u,v]^{T}\\,:\\,x-2v=0,\\,u=0\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Solving $x-2v=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=2v.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Ker}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2v \\\\ y \\\\ z \\\\ 0 \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}+v{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=3.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =5-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =5-3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef4c3240-efcc-5031-be97-b18f24fa624c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477283} {:answer/id #uuid "80eef841-b3bb-539f-a20d-b7af5fdc129f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef4c3240-efcc-5031-be97-b18f24fa624c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477283} :answer-field/id #uuid "97b22468-5364-5814-ab56-00d3827125a4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477611} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ddb04cf-498a-5aa6-bd12-7e6b3799bde4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477285} {:answer/id #uuid "6462c36b-1577-5e5f-980f-afbd1f21ace1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6462c36b-1577-5e5f-980f-afbd1f21ace1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477286} :answer-field/id #uuid "f417e3d1-8fa9-576f-97f4-84f0c7ed2417" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477612} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7971526-d720-5bf2-916b-1c3268cfc3a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477287} {:answer/id #uuid "ca68341f-f266-5714-a2d3-03c3de4d3096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7971526-d720-5bf2-916b-1c3268cfc3a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477287} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf2f78e9-40f7-51c7-9823-236bb1c047ce" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e86ac40c-9f96-593f-88c6-aef65bbc3b17" :question/math-academy-id "q-38136" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in ${R}^{5}.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$, then $\\text{__________}.$ *\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(T)=1$\n- **II.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$\n- **III.** $T$ is a bijection\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=5.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =5-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =5-3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b54b975-97e0-5630-a090-aee094b6f975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492343} {:answer/id #uuid "8198ad48-3fde-56ad-875f-2629a469e0c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492479} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc05555-6a06-512b-99ec-1c0211b082fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492525} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c9c60f-e173-5835-8474-cd48d43dde3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492552} {:answer/id #uuid "da447d07-58e2-5574-841d-f2cf464005db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b54b975-97e0-5630-a090-aee094b6f975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492343} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ef148dd-6753-5ef9-9d57-5a47223ec593" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "52e3deff-a33f-5133-be77-3539d124a1dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-38146" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation with standard matrix $T$ whose columns are ${v}_{1},$\n ${v}_{2},$ and  ${v}_{3}.$\n\n*If  $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=1$, then $\\text{__________}.$ *\n\nWhich of the following, if inserted into the blank space, would lead to a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution\n\nIII. $\\text{rank}(T)=2$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{dim}(\\text{span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=1$ this means that $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$ According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the invertible matrix theorem the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0$ is equivalent to $Tx=0.$ Since $T$ is not invertible, the equation has non-trivial solutions.\n\n- Statement III is false. From statement I, $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a9a16cc-d807-5015-ba40-b4a15fe3a0db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Given a Description of the Image or Kernel"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61e472bd-ec33-5a3e-8b54-62a2891a9c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not enough information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366147} {:answer/id #uuid "a5dc371b-1396-5e61-a5b7-e3d432e28bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366148} {:answer/id #uuid "eca1d3e3-4905-5d15-ac5c-4cc6e7addee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366149} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4ff1b0-90ec-51f1-9adc-f5e7ecc391d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366150} {:answer/id #uuid "f218d39c-ce4c-5b79-8c33-1b7543b0b540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc4ff1b0-90ec-51f1-9adc-f5e7ecc391d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366150} :answer-field/id #uuid "319ca848-ea15-555c-8b75-4d09284cc695" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09a30319-539f-5865-a29f-fd523154b963" :question/math-academy-id "q-47468" :question/problem "Find $\\text{nullity}(T)$ given that $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ and \n\n$$\n\\text{Im}(T)=\\{[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,-x+z=0\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Solving $-x+z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=x.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc062b6-306b-5edc-81db-1a3051cf3f51" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c9f16ce-fef4-521e-aa19-909f1091f6bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187963} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff61651-2e13-54a4-b210-5bed2056dad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187964} {:answer/id #uuid "1c653d92-380c-5b32-bad5-9eabf3045e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187965} {:answer/id #uuid "64f16583-db95-5616-ba6e-96d47b247b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187966} {:answer/id #uuid "77d171d6-6d3d-5964-ba40-67f525bff66d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ff61651-2e13-54a4-b210-5bed2056dad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187964} :answer-field/id #uuid "6340f7a9-a35b-51f1-bf15-ace1f76e6505" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "710bfb29-d88f-5f8e-82d2-27ffc4845463" :question/math-academy-id "q-83444" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T:{R}^{7}\\to {R}^{7}.$ If $\\text{rank}(T)=3$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given transformation, we have $n=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(T) & =7 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(T) & =7-3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a9a16cc-d807-5015-ba40-b4a15fe3a0db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Given a Description of the Image or Kernel"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1470852b-03e9-5db4-8d33-ce40b9b312ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not enough information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406812} {:answer/id #uuid "a80e2855-a004-5120-bf2a-4803a51aedf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406821} {:answer/id #uuid "af65c1f2-8ae8-5564-a484-7a9735e719d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406822} {:answer/id #uuid "c038e33b-b13d-5ebb-aa6f-234d43c1d51b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406825} {:answer/id #uuid "e9530934-50fc-57c8-a93e-515a75115d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9530934-50fc-57c8-a93e-515a75115d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406830} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c728edd-0030-5f84-bc57-b18190d3fb0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94b1ad7b-577b-5c7c-8164-0f25bc8b5263" :question/math-academy-id "q-83446" :question/problem "Find $\\text{rank}(T)$ given that $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ and $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,x+y+z=0\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Solving $x+y+z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=-x-y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Ker}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ -x-y\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc062b6-306b-5edc-81db-1a3051cf3f51" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ece1630-5fc9-5fa3-bff5-c3877dbe20e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492243} {:answer/id #uuid "31a74265-ac02-58e5-96a3-8af498759acb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492322} {:answer/id #uuid "991369ce-452a-5ef2-95ba-e3a495f00ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492514} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b11d96-fc0e-5fe3-ba6f-917cc5262380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492551} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f5c64c-d239-5fc6-a10c-ef12ea321845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31a74265-ac02-58e5-96a3-8af498759acb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492322} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6a1b3a9-cf8a-56c9-b44f-3a84bdac3d50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0e7b715-b8f5-546c-8370-11eb96261ecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-84054" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T:{R}^{6}\\to {R}^{6}.$ If $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given transformation, we have $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(T)+0 & =6 \\\\ \\text{rank}(T) & =6-0 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a9a16cc-d807-5015-ba40-b4a15fe3a0db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Given a Description of the Image or Kernel"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92c91297-c7c0-5bdc-b9d9-a9a52ae806c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187969} {:answer/id #uuid "99b215f4-c062-59cc-b003-ea6ef57c1e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187970} {:answer/id #uuid "b7165ef5-c1ee-52e1-97ed-92ea885bd55a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187971} {:answer/id #uuid "0874d691-cb56-565c-9d14-cf75209b28ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187972} {:answer/id #uuid "22fb8de2-7841-5738-b5c1-945f57d8818f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7165ef5-c1ee-52e1-97ed-92ea885bd55a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187971} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7e4fdbb-a7f0-534a-a976-d0c43448ef20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f05e62e2-1597-5312-8f1a-20a85ca4f47f" :question/math-academy-id "q-84059" :question/problem "Find $\\text{rank}(T)$ given that $T:{R}^{4}\\to {R}^{4}$ and \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{[x,y,z,w]^{T}\\,:\\,x+y+z=0,\\,2x-y-w=0\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Solving $x+y+z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=-x-y.$ Solving $2x-y-w=0$ for $w,$ we get $w=2x-y.$  Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Ker}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ 2x-y \\\\ -x-y\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =4-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f245e34-a77e-597f-a14d-caed4ce72982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492438} {:answer/id #uuid "75c34ea7-fced-573f-ac33-5467b58565b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492454} {:answer/id #uuid "96f2b9de-077d-5cf3-a270-01b08efe97fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492509} {:answer/id #uuid "af2b2ba4-b180-5b0a-b84d-e9a4e5bcda0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492567} {:answer/id #uuid "e046b8e1-07f1-59f1-8371-d26dc2d3848e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96f2b9de-077d-5cf3-a270-01b08efe97fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492509} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5c9d97c-c13f-5d1d-806f-0620d8934268" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e76412ff-223a-5baa-b8c0-73f134709b3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-84061" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=3$\n\nII. If $v=[1,0,0]^{T}$ is a solution of $T(x)=0$ then $T$ is invertible.\n\nIII. If there is not $u\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(u)=[1,0,0]^{T},$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=3.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & \\ne 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $v$ is a solution of $T(x)=0$, we have that $T(v)=0.$ Therefore $v\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ thus according to the invertible matrix theorem $T$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since there is not $u\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(u)=[1,0,0]^{T},$ we have that $T$ is not  a bijection (an invertible transformation). According to the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07f285c5-f28d-52d7-8edb-dc3cd9308f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451845} {:answer/id #uuid "de316b3c-8465-5f0f-9e1d-c901b94e2cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de316b3c-8465-5f0f-9e1d-c901b94e2cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451846} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bbe03ec-cd1a-5f9e-bdaa-621344b8730a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452770} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c2821d1-dbdf-57db-8669-559772eabf32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451847} {:answer/id #uuid "0c7b316c-fd27-57d7-ae6d-1a8dea1ee57f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c7b316c-fd27-57d7-ae6d-1a8dea1ee57f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451848} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecdec4e8-476e-5e64-bfe1-002886cf49b8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452771} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e26320c-f5fc-5d22-962b-bc6fcc8e7c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451849} {:answer/id #uuid "fd978c93-8391-56ee-b944-a6b07dbbf21d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd978c93-8391-56ee-b944-a6b07dbbf21d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451850} :answer-field/id #uuid "38dc4bf6-46bb-5bfd-8850-b313313b3744" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c81c0d34-9745-5e75-9229-e0ad0c633344" :question/math-academy-id "q-84063" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,x=0}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$\n- **II.** If $\\text{Ker}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y-2z=0,x+z=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$\n- **III.** If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Solving $y-2z=0$ for $y,$ we get $y=2z.$ Solving $x+z=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-z.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-z \\\\ 2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=z{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If the columns of $T$ are such that ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2},$ then the set of vectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent and therefore $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Now, according to the matrix invertible theorem, we obtain that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}]}
